'''Punjabi''' (also '''Panjabi'''; {{lang|pa|ਪੰਜਾਬੀ}} ''{{IAST|Paṁjābī}}'' in [[Gurmukhi script|Gurmukhī]], {{lang|ur|پنجابی}} ''Panjābī'' in [[Shahmukhi|Shāhmukhī]]) is the language of the [[Punjabis|Punjabi people]] of the [[Punjab region]]s of [[India]] and [[Pakistan]]. It is an [[Indo-European language]] of the [[Indo-Iranian languages|Indo-Iranian]] subfamily. It is an [[Agglutinative language|agglutinative]] and [[tonal language|tonal]] language where words are ordered '[[Subject Object Verb]]'.

The Punjabi people suffered a split between India and Pakistan during the [[Partition_of_India|Partition]] of [[1947]]. However, Punjabi language and culture tend to be uniting factors in spite of national and religious affiliations.

==Dialects and geographic distribution==

Punjabi is the official language of the Indian state of Punjab and it is one of the second official languages of [[Delhi]].&lt;ref&gt;[http://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/articleshow/msid-43388,prtpage-1.cms The Times of India - &quot;Punjabi, Urdu made official languages in Delhi&quot;] [[25 June]] [[2003]]&lt;/ref&gt;  It is also spoken in neighbouring areas such as [[Haryana]] and [[Himachal Pradesh]].  Punjabi is the predominant spoken language in the [[Punjab (Pakistan)|Punjab province]] of [[Pakistan]] although it has no official status there, and both [[Urdu]] and [[English language|English]] are preferred languages of the elite.

Punjabi is also spoken as a [[minority language]] in several other countries where [[Punjabis]] have emigrated in large numbers, such as [[United Kingdom|Britain]], the [[United States]], [[Australia]] and especially [[Canada]], where it is the fifth most commonly used language.&lt;ref&gt;[http://www12.statcan.ca/english/census01/products/standard/themes/RetrieveProductTable.cfm?Temporal=2001&amp;PID=55536&amp;APATH=3&amp;GID=431515&amp;METH=1&amp;PTYPE=55440&amp;THEME=41&amp;FOCUS=0&amp;AID=0&amp;PLACENAME=0&amp;PROVINCE=0&amp;SEARCH=0&amp;GC=0&amp;GK=0&amp;VID=0&amp;FL=0&amp;RL=0&amp;FREE=0 Canadian Census Data (2001)]&lt;/ref&gt;  Punjabi is the preferred language of the [[Sikhism|Sikhs]], in which some of their [[Guru_Granth_Sahib|religious literature]] is written. It is the usual language of [[Bhangra]] music, which has recently gained wide popularity both in [[South Asia]] and abroad.

There are many dialects of Punjabi and they all form part of a [[dialect continuum]], merging with [[Sindhi]] and related languages in Pakistan, and [[Hindustani]] in India.  The main dialects of Punjabi are Majhi, Doabi, Malwai and Powadhi in India, and Pothohari, Lahndi and Multani in Pakistan.  Majhi is the standard written form of Punjabi and is the dialect used in both [[Amritsar]] and [[Lahore]].

The [[Punjabi University]], [[Patiala]], lists the following as dialects of Punjabi:&lt;ref&gt;[http://www.advancedcentrepunjabi.org/intro1.asp Advanced Centre for Technical Development of Punjabi  Language, Literature and Culture]&lt;/ref&gt;

