      <text xml:space="preserve">{{GBdot|Padstow - Cornwall}}
{{For|the [[Sydney]] suburb|Padstow, New South Wales}}
'''Padstow''' ([[Cornish language|Cornish]]: Lannwedhenek) is a small town in the western reaches of the [[Bristol Channel]] on the north coast of [[Cornwall]], [[England]], approximately fourteen [[mile]]s east of [[Newquay]], at the mouth of the [[River Camel]] ({{gbmapping|SW919754}}). Traditionally a [[fishing]] port, it is now a popular [[tourist destination]], although some of its former fishing fleet remains, it is mainly a yachting haven on a dramatic coastline with few easily navigable harbours. Note the old sailor's maxim &quot;Twixt Hartland Point and Padstow Bay is a sailor's grave by night or day.&quot; The [[United Kingdom Census 2001|UK Census 2001]] reported a [[parish]] [[population]] of 3,162{{ref|population}}.

It was originally named &quot;Petroc-stow&quot;, after the Welsh missionary St. [[Petroc]], who landed at nearby [[Trebetherick]] around [[Anno Domini|AD]] [[500]]. More recently, some have nicknamed it &quot;Padstein&quot; on account of resident TV chef [[Rick Stein]] running a number of restaurants and hotels in the town. The most most well-known of these is the ''Seafood Restaurant'' on the quayside.


== Transport ==
[[Image:Padstow_1.jpg|thumb|right|250px|Padstow harbour and quayside]]
Between [[1899]] and [[1967]] the [[Bodmin]]-[[Wadebridge]] railway extended to Padstow. The old railway line is now the Camel Trail [http://www.cameltrail.com], a footpath and cycle path popular due to its picturesque route beside the River Camel. On the other side of the river, a ferry runs between Padstow and [[Rock, Cornwall|Rock]].

During the mid-[[19th century|nineteenth century]], ships carrying timber from [[Canada]] (particularly [[Quebec]]) would arrive at Padstow and offer cheap travel to passengers wishing to emigrate. Shipbuilders in the area would also benefit from the quality of their cargoes. Among the ships that sailed were the [[barque]]s ''[[Clio (barque)|Clio]]'', ''[[Belle (barque)|Belle]]'' [http://ist.uwaterloo.ca/~marj/genealogy/ships/ships1843.html] and ''[[Voluna (barque)|Voluna]]''; and the [[brig]] ''[[Dalusia (brig)|Dalusia]]'' [http://freepages.history.rootsweb.com/~biblechristian/other/eynon_john_voyage.html].
&lt;br clear=all&gt;
=='Obby 'Oss festival==
[[Image:Red_obby_oss_maiden_20050502.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The black and red 'Obby 'Oss capturing a passing maiden during the Mayday festival.]]
Padstow is best known for its &quot;'Obby 'Oss&quot; festival. Although its origins are unclear, it most likely stems from an ancient [[fertility rite]], perhaps the [[Celtic mythology|Celtic festival]] of [[Beltane]]. The festival starts at midnight on [[May Day|May Eve]] when townspeople sing the &quot;Morning Song&quot;. In the morning, the town is dressed with greenery and flowers are placed around a [[maypole]]. The climax arrives when male dancers cavort through the town dressed as one of two 'Obby 'Osses, the &quot;Old&quot; and the &quot;Blue Ribbon&quot; 'Obby 'Osses; as the name suggests, they are stylised kinds of horses. Prodded on by [[acolyte]]s known as &quot;Teasers&quot;, each wears a gruesome mask and black frame-hung cape under which they try to catch young maidens as they pass through the town. Finally, at midnight on May Day, the crowd sings of the 'Obby 'Oss death, until its resurrection the following May Eve.
[[Image:Red_obby_oss_party_20050502.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The red party attending the 'Obby 'Oss with dozens of accordians and drums.]]

== External links ==

====Pubs and Bars In Padstow====
